WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:13.016 --> 00:00:16.305
So what do people usually say
when you're about to give a public talk?

00:00:17.159 --> 00:00:19.627
It's to imagine
that your audience is naked.

00:00:19.651 --> 00:00:21.420
(Laughter)

00:00:21.444 --> 00:00:23.585
Well, I'm doing a different trick tonight,

00:00:23.609 --> 00:00:27.156
and I'm going to imagine
all of us without farmers,

00:00:27.180 --> 00:00:29.871
and well, it's not so much different.

00:00:29.895 --> 00:00:32.324
[Without farmers you'd be hungry,
naked and sober]

00:00:32.348 --> 00:00:33.349
(Laughter)

00:00:33.373 --> 00:00:36.395
And our farmers do so much more for us

00:00:36.419 --> 00:00:41.307
than simply feed and clothe
and provide us excellent things to drink.

00:00:41.776 --> 00:00:45.461
Our farmers are an important part
of all of our communities,

00:00:45.485 --> 00:00:47.818
particularly our rural communities.

00:00:48.334 --> 00:00:50.167
And more than that,

00:00:50.191 --> 00:00:53.968
they're a strong driver
of resilient economics.

00:00:54.739 --> 00:00:56.219
Think about it this way:

00:00:56.243 --> 00:00:59.672
When a brewer buys hops from me,
grown here in Minnesota,

00:00:59.696 --> 00:01:02.044
90 percent of that dollar
stays in our state,

00:01:02.068 --> 00:01:05.188
compared to just 10 percent
when they buy it from somewhere else.

00:01:05.212 --> 00:01:07.077
What that means is a lot.

00:01:07.101 --> 00:01:09.403
That 90 percent means local jobs.

00:01:09.427 --> 00:01:12.370
It means tax revenue
for better schools and roads.

00:01:12.394 --> 00:01:14.672
It means support
for the co-ops, the mechanics,

00:01:14.696 --> 00:01:18.212
all the support staff that are needed
for a farm to thrive.

00:01:18.743 --> 00:01:20.776
And they're our best stewards of the land.

00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:24.900
This quote, I think, exemplifies
what our family farmers do for us

00:01:24.924 --> 00:01:27.402
in stewarding our shared
natural resources.

00:01:28.140 --> 00:01:33.987
"That land is a community
is the basic concept of ecology,

00:01:34.011 --> 00:01:38.230
but that land is to be loved and respected
as an extension of ethics."

00:01:39.628 --> 00:01:43.079
Now, they sure do
a lot of good stuff for us.

00:01:43.714 --> 00:01:46.355
And our family farmers
are great, we'd all agree.

00:01:46.379 --> 00:01:48.291
However,

00:01:48.315 --> 00:01:50.759
the trends in agriculture today are dire.

00:01:50.783 --> 00:01:52.664
The average age of a farmer in America,

00:01:52.688 --> 00:01:55.410
according to the latest
agricultural census --

00:01:55.434 --> 00:01:56.934
58.3.

00:01:56.958 --> 00:01:58.886
Of all the farmers,

00:01:58.910 --> 00:02:01.369
33 percent are 65 plus.

00:02:01.393 --> 00:02:03.379
That's a little caricature of my grandpa.

00:02:03.403 --> 00:02:04.727
(Laughter)

00:02:04.751 --> 00:02:06.348
He's still farming,

00:02:06.372 --> 00:02:08.506
and he's much older than 65.

00:02:08.530 --> 00:02:10.007
But to put that in perspective,

00:02:10.031 --> 00:02:12.862
another important
public service job, teaching,

00:02:12.886 --> 00:02:14.887
average age of teachers is 42.

00:02:15.950 --> 00:02:18.283
Our farmers are pretty old
in this country.

00:02:18.307 --> 00:02:20.268
And unfortunately,

00:02:20.292 --> 00:02:23.862
when they retire, if they retire,

00:02:23.886 --> 00:02:25.768
we're not really replacing them.

00:02:25.792 --> 00:02:28.053
Of all the farmers
that we added in this country

00:02:28.077 --> 00:02:30.576
between 2008 and 2012,

00:02:30.600 --> 00:02:32.695
across the entire United States --

00:02:32.719 --> 00:02:34.613
see if you can catch this difference --

00:02:34.637 --> 00:02:37.369
we added 2,000 under the age of 30.

00:02:37.393 --> 00:02:39.402
I'm one of those.

00:02:39.426 --> 00:02:42.315
I'll be around to autograph
some photos later, if you'd like.

00:02:42.339 --> 00:02:44.363
(Laughter)

00:02:44.665 --> 00:02:46.769
But, you know, our farmers
are getting older

00:02:46.793 --> 00:02:48.309
and we're not replacing them --

00:02:48.333 --> 00:02:49.625
what's going on here?

00:02:49.649 --> 00:02:51.037
What are we going to do?

00:02:51.061 --> 00:02:53.760
And I think there's a reason
folks aren't coming into it,

00:02:53.784 --> 00:02:54.943
and that's prices.

00:02:54.967 --> 00:02:57.545
We're going to go through
a couple of slides like this.

00:02:57.569 --> 00:03:01.346
Milk: This is the average retail price
of a gallon of milk in the United States.

00:03:01.370 --> 00:03:02.821
Four dollars forty-nine cents.

00:03:02.845 --> 00:03:04.657
How much do you think the farmer gets?

00:03:04.681 --> 00:03:06.778
Dollar thirty-two.

00:03:06.802 --> 00:03:08.133
We'll try again with bread.

00:03:08.157 --> 00:03:10.934
Average retail price of bread
in America, three forty-nine.

00:03:10.958 --> 00:03:12.271
Farmer gets ...

00:03:12.295 --> 00:03:13.446
Twelve cents.

00:03:13.470 --> 00:03:14.621
Audience: Oh!

00:03:14.645 --> 00:03:17.795
And so how are we supposed to have
strong local farms

00:03:19.342 --> 00:03:20.512
in this scenario?

00:03:20.536 --> 00:03:24.059
What are we supposed to do
if there aren't any local farmers left?

00:03:25.206 --> 00:03:27.699
And this isn't just a farmer problem,

00:03:27.723 --> 00:03:30.910
it's not just something
for the few of us farmers to sort out.

00:03:30.934 --> 00:03:32.323
This is an all-of-us problem.

00:03:32.347 --> 00:03:35.775
This is rural and it's urban
and it's statewide and it's nationwide.

00:03:35.799 --> 00:03:37.533
So what do we do about it?

00:03:38.426 --> 00:03:39.656
I'll tell you that.

00:03:39.680 --> 00:03:40.947
But first, a story.

00:03:41.879 --> 00:03:44.721
The green movement,
we're all kind of familiar,

00:03:44.745 --> 00:03:46.927
started in the '60s, planting trees.

00:03:47.417 --> 00:03:49.188
And now we've come such a long way.

00:03:49.212 --> 00:03:51.488
Green is part of our day-to-day lives.

00:03:51.512 --> 00:03:54.520
It's part of the day-to-day lives
of Fortune 500 businesses.

00:03:54.544 --> 00:03:56.561
It's the subject
of international treaties,

00:03:56.585 --> 00:03:58.696
the subject of presidential debates.

00:03:59.125 --> 00:04:01.077
You and I, we switch our light bulbs,

00:04:01.101 --> 00:04:03.272
we use reusable bags.

00:04:03.744 --> 00:04:06.403
We participate in the green movement
each and every day.

00:04:06.427 --> 00:04:07.760
Yet ...

00:04:07.784 --> 00:04:10.514
and this is how we get to the idea --

00:04:10.538 --> 00:04:11.689
the food movement,

00:04:11.713 --> 00:04:15.093
relatively younger,
but also somewhat familiar, I imagine.

00:04:15.117 --> 00:04:16.474
You go to the grocery store,

00:04:16.498 --> 00:04:18.307
you see a sign that says "Buy local,"

00:04:18.331 --> 00:04:20.950
you go to the farmers market,
you go to the co-op,

00:04:20.974 --> 00:04:23.238
you read books by prominent authors.

00:04:23.903 --> 00:04:25.117
The food movement to date

00:04:25.141 --> 00:04:28.029
could be summarized
as voting with your fork.

00:04:28.053 --> 00:04:30.592
The idea is: you pull a dollar
out of your wallet --

00:04:30.616 --> 00:04:32.958
how you spend that dollar
affects the food system.

00:04:32.982 --> 00:04:35.249
It supports farmers close to home.

00:04:35.974 --> 00:04:38.482
And that's all well and good,
but where are we going?

00:04:38.506 --> 00:04:40.570
How do we get to our
renewable-energy moment

00:04:40.594 --> 00:04:41.934
like the green movement did?

00:04:41.958 --> 00:04:44.147
And this, I think, is what we need to do.

00:04:44.521 --> 00:04:47.409
Just voting with our fork
is not solving the issues

00:04:47.433 --> 00:04:48.782
that our farmers are facing.

00:04:48.806 --> 00:04:50.560
And so we need to do more than that.

00:04:50.584 --> 00:04:53.497
I believe we must move on
from just voting with our fork

00:04:53.521 --> 00:04:55.397
to voting with our vote.

00:04:55.421 --> 00:04:58.278
We need to take our dollars

00:04:58.302 --> 00:04:59.987
and continue to spend them locally.

00:05:00.011 --> 00:05:02.744
We also need to show up
at the ballot box for our farmers.

00:05:03.167 --> 00:05:05.516
This is bigger than just
buying local strawberries

00:05:05.540 --> 00:05:07.317
once a year at a pick-your-own.

00:05:07.341 --> 00:05:09.913
This is a year-round effort
that we must make together

00:05:09.937 --> 00:05:11.324
to make the change we need.

00:05:11.793 --> 00:05:14.324
Changes like fair pricing for farmers.

00:05:14.348 --> 00:05:16.579
That's quotas, supply management,

00:05:16.603 --> 00:05:17.803
guaranteed prices.

00:05:18.792 --> 00:05:21.197
Changes like fair and open trade.

00:05:21.221 --> 00:05:23.155
That means ending trade wars.

00:05:23.499 --> 00:05:26.300
And yeah, of course it means voting.

00:05:26.324 --> 00:05:28.277
Now we all knew that one already, though.

00:05:28.301 --> 00:05:29.562
For example, it's working.

00:05:29.586 --> 00:05:31.260
Hey, who's that?

00:05:31.284 --> 00:05:32.958
(Laughter)

00:05:32.982 --> 00:05:35.061
Just this year in Minnesota,

00:05:35.085 --> 00:05:37.760
we've passed a historic,
first-in-the-country tax credit.

00:05:37.784 --> 00:05:39.917
The Beginning Farmer Tax Credit.

00:05:40.323 --> 00:05:42.522
It incentivizes our transition of land

00:05:42.546 --> 00:05:45.561
from the existing generation
to the next generation.

00:05:46.125 --> 00:05:48.752
That was done by a handful
of us young farmers --

00:05:48.776 --> 00:05:51.220
we certainly don't have money,
you saw that earlier.

00:05:51.569 --> 00:05:53.276
We don't have political experience.

00:05:53.300 --> 00:05:55.530
But we showed up,
and we made our voices heard.

00:05:55.554 --> 00:05:58.434
And thanks to the support
of farmers and non-farmers alike,

00:05:58.458 --> 00:06:00.992
we got something incredible done
here in this state.

00:06:02.538 --> 00:06:04.482
If we can do it, anybody can do it.

00:06:04.506 --> 00:06:07.791
Now, that was all light and fuzzy
and feels pretty happy.

00:06:07.815 --> 00:06:10.092
Skeptics in the audience, you're here.

00:06:10.116 --> 00:06:11.737
That would be me, if I were here.

00:06:11.761 --> 00:06:12.983
Skeptics are thinking,

00:06:13.007 --> 00:06:15.988
"Wow, what do we need to change
about our food system?"

00:06:16.704 --> 00:06:17.904
Farmers are great.

00:06:18.260 --> 00:06:20.593
We have unlimited food,
and it's real cheap, too,

00:06:20.617 --> 00:06:21.767
isn't that great?

00:06:22.450 --> 00:06:23.696
Well, unfortunately,

00:06:23.720 --> 00:06:27.998
in the '80s and the '90s in this country,
we went down a path of policy

00:06:28.022 --> 00:06:30.855
that could be described
as "get big or get out."

00:06:31.926 --> 00:06:35.696
And what "get big or get out" means
is you maximize production

00:06:35.720 --> 00:06:37.254
while minimizing costs.

00:06:37.792 --> 00:06:40.228
On its face value,
that sounds pretty simple.

00:06:40.530 --> 00:06:46.458
However, that shift turned
our farmers from a venerated class

00:06:46.482 --> 00:06:48.228
and a valued class in our society

00:06:48.252 --> 00:06:50.119
into a cost to be minimized.

00:06:51.355 --> 00:06:54.006
That shift made it
so that my great-grandfather,

00:06:54.030 --> 00:06:57.286
who supported the family with six cows,

00:06:57.310 --> 00:06:59.204
that same dairy,

00:06:59.228 --> 00:07:02.117
trying to support their family,
has to be 600 cows today.

00:07:02.141 --> 00:07:06.188
Six-thousand-cow dairies
are not unheard of.

00:07:07.446 --> 00:07:09.549
What happens when
there's this one dairy farm

00:07:09.573 --> 00:07:10.748
in an entire county,

00:07:10.772 --> 00:07:12.374
where there used to be hundreds?

00:07:12.954 --> 00:07:16.454
The same could be said
with corn or beans or field crops.

00:07:17.116 --> 00:07:20.815
What happens when it takes 10,000 acres
for one person to support themselves?

00:07:21.871 --> 00:07:23.804
When it used to only take 40.

00:07:25.038 --> 00:07:27.951
We know what happens,
we read about it in the news.

00:07:28.448 --> 00:07:30.068
Broadly determined, rural decline,

00:07:30.092 --> 00:07:31.983
but schools close, schools consolidate,

00:07:32.007 --> 00:07:34.713
post offices close, grocery stores close.

00:07:35.649 --> 00:07:36.847
People leave,

00:07:36.871 --> 00:07:39.271
the community suffers and goes away.

00:07:40.109 --> 00:07:43.204
I believe all of us in this audience
with ties to rural Minnesota

00:07:43.228 --> 00:07:44.628
know this story well.

00:07:46.117 --> 00:07:50.998
This is not a problem that we can solve
with farmers markets and good intentions.

00:07:51.022 --> 00:07:53.608
We have to do more for our farmers.

00:07:53.632 --> 00:07:55.895
Policy got us into this mess,

00:07:55.919 --> 00:07:57.652
and policy can get us out.

00:07:59.109 --> 00:08:03.781
American farmers are only getting
older, fewer and poorer,

00:08:04.163 --> 00:08:06.430
yet they are crucial to our state.

00:08:06.782 --> 00:08:09.186
They're the vibrancy
in our rural communities.

00:08:09.210 --> 00:08:12.552
They're the drivers
of economic growth and stability,

00:08:12.576 --> 00:08:16.354
and they are our best protectors
of our shared resources

00:08:16.378 --> 00:08:17.911
of land, water and air.

00:08:18.268 --> 00:08:20.307
So we have to do better for them.

00:08:20.331 --> 00:08:21.798
So join me, would you?

00:08:22.220 --> 00:08:23.728
Let's fight for our farmers.

00:08:23.752 --> 00:08:24.903
You can see it,

00:08:24.927 --> 00:08:28.082
we're already doing it in Minnesota,
having great success.

00:08:28.736 --> 00:08:30.569
And together, we can do even more.

00:08:30.593 --> 00:08:31.815
And we must.

00:08:31.839 --> 00:08:35.154
So we were voting with our fork before,

00:08:35.178 --> 00:08:36.970
and we want to keep doing that.

00:08:36.994 --> 00:08:39.877
But if I could have one idea
for you to go home with today,

00:08:39.901 --> 00:08:41.702
it's vote with your vote.

00:08:41.726 --> 00:08:42.996
And so to that end,

00:08:43.020 --> 00:08:46.094
on the count of three,
I'd like all of us to say it together.

00:08:46.118 --> 00:08:47.268
Are you ready?

00:08:48.477 --> 00:08:50.445
OK, one,

00:08:50.469 --> 00:08:51.865
two,

00:08:51.889 --> 00:08:53.052
three.

00:08:53.076 --> 00:08:55.301
Audience: Vote with your vote.

00:08:55.325 --> 00:08:56.492
Very nice, thank you.

00:08:56.516 --> 00:08:57.786
I think you got it.

00:08:57.810 --> 00:09:00.337
(Applause)

